of all the things i expected from eldon  ring a sense of serenity wasn't one of  them whether you've played from  software's games or simply aware of them  you likely have an idea of what they  offer doomed kingdoms tragic characters  a pervasive sense of melancholy and  gameplay that will challenge both your  skill and resolve and yet what defined  my seven hours of hands-on time with the  elden ring closed network test was what  many would consider uncharacteristic of  a soulsborne game  tranquility  as much of a surprise as that was it's  not something that's completely uncommon  in these games whether it's the nexus  filing shrine hunter's dream or the  dilapidated temple each from soft title  has a place where adventurers can find  respite from the cruelty of the world  beyond these places have a sympathetic  quality to them somehow being able to  acknowledge the harshness of the world  lament that its fate has been put upon  your shoulders and comfort you when  you're at your lowest giving you the  space to put yourself together before  you pick yourself up and venture out  once more walking around the lands  between specifically a small section of  an area called lingrave i felt that same  intent but laid out over a vast open  world the gentle understated music that  played as i wandered around aimlessly or  marveled at the beautiful golden archery  towering above invited me to let my  guard down and take in the world around  me without fear that a blade would  suddenly be plunged into me and when i  did i found the world to be both  familiar and unlike anything in from  software's previous games the lands  between is undoubtedly yet another place  ruled by figures that have been tainted  by other worldly power and corrupted but  it's also a beautiful place teeming with  life that came through strongest when i  opened the map picked a location that  interested me and attempted to reach it  along the way i stopped on the edge of  cliffs and gazed out into the horizon as  day turned to night i watched animals  like goats and penguins or whatever  their fantasy equivalents are in this  world just being for some reason i was  oddly fascinated with birds which didn't  attack me when i approached as i  expected instead they simply took flight  and landed elsewhere then there was the  sound of the wild crashing ocean waves  whispering winds rustling trees the  pitter patter of raindrops it was  hypnotic  this is all incredibly basic stuff for  an open world game perhaps even  unremarkable given the prevalence in  similar types of games but in a souls  born game these sounds are more unusual  than multi-armed monsters wielding  swords the reason it stuck with me is  because as the tarnished you're not  walking in the ashes of a fallen kingdom  or doomed city but fighting to change  the fate of a place that is very much  still alive and that is something new  for from software it's nothing short of  a miracle  that the guidance of grace can lead you  maidenless as you are to the perfect  place to die in obscurity elden ring it  seems is a game built around both  exploration and discovery the setting of  the lands between feels like a place  that is designed to constantly pique  your curiosity and then reward you for  seeking out answers in my time with the  game i found all manner of interesting  things unprompted underground crypts  that held new weapons caves so dark that  each step forward felt like a perilous  risk and yes that classic from software  moment where i ventured into the unknown  and emerged in the familiar showing that  the world was constructed thoughtfully  truth be told i'm certain that i didn't  see all of what most would call that  main events in the demo because i just  wanted to poke around in different parts  of the map this thankfully was made  incredibly easy thanks to the fast  travel system from software has  implemented from the very outset i was  able to move between sites of grace the  game's equivalent of bonfires as i  discovered them eldon ring allows you to  progress time at the sights of grace too  so i kept checking out different areas  at different times of the day to see if  anything changed they didn't hear but  they may do in the full release or i  would do so just to get a sense of how  it felt to be there as the state of the  world shifted needless to say i lost  myself in the game before i'd even  crossed swords with a challenging boss  but cross swords i did and it was pretty  much exactly what i wanted from the game  for better or worse depending on your  perspective the combat oriented gameplay  in eldon ring feels like a dark souls  game i mentioned dark souls specifically  because bloodborne and sakura shadows  die twice have distinct pacing feel and  mechanics that distinguish them elden  ring is built within the framework of  souls albeit with some changes here and  there the most noticeable of these is  the ability to jump which not only makes  hopping around for traversal  considerably easier and more reliable  but also introduces some interesting new  wrinkles to combat naturally leaping  away from danger is now a viable escape  tactic but its use in offense is likely  where most will get mileage from the  jump launching at an enemy and executing  a heavy attack will deal a crushing blow  that breaks the target's stance opening  them up for follow-ups provided you've  got the stamina to do so the other major  new addition is the guard counter which  is executed by using a heavy attack  immediately after guarding when done  properly this will also break an enemy's  stance it felt like a satisfying way to  turn defense into offense quickly but it  also requires some thought as many  enemies especially mini bosses and  bosses will have follow-up attacks of  their own and may even shrug off  incoming damage from you to deliver  those picking the right moment and  employing a solid strategy is still  important when using a guard counter  this i feel has the potential to switch  up the dynamics of combat in the same  way that counters with the gun in  bloodborne did but without the strict  execution window they demanded i spent  the majority of my time using the bloody  wolf class and by default it had a  broadsword and shield the latter of  which allowed me to execute the parry  and riposte combo interestingly  different equipment can often bestow  different abilities too using their  sword my character was able to summon a  bolt of lightning that struck targeted  enemies to deal big damage at the cost  of some of my focus point bar later i  stumbled upon a cavalcade of soldiers  protecting a carriage being dragged by  two giants throwing caution to the wind  i decided to engage initially crouching  to sneak into nearby foliage and hide  approaching when safe to backstab a  specific enemy that looked like trouble  once they were onto me i created some  distance and began picking off the  approaching soldiers with my lightning  strikes taking out any that got close by  swinging my sword the giants meanwhile  required a new strategy elden ring gives  players the ability to summon a steed  called torrent more of an extremely  capable goat than a horse and in turn  this enables mounted combat my strategy  for the giants given their massive reach  and devastating power was hit and run  which worked very effectively even if it  did take a while mounted combat felt  cumbersome at first but was able to  eventually get comfortable by adopting  different strategies depending on the  size of the enemy for larger enemies  like the giants or other mounted  opponents locking on and strafing around  them was the way to go however moving  while attempting to hit smaller on-foot  enemies proved to be very unwieldy in  most cases though i could simply stop  and use the knockback from my attacks to  keep me safe one of the smarter  additions to the souls formula in eldon  ring comes in the form of rewards for  defeating enemy mobs one-on-one combat  against enemies in the open world is  very simple and i suspect won't be a  challenge for many players so as not to  make combat in the open world trivial  from software here has also peppered in  groups of enemies which prove to be a  much greater challenge some like strange  bat-like creatures will take the  opportunity to swarm you others like  soldiers will alert nearby troops and a  skirmish against a handful of enemies  can quickly escalate into a battle  against multiple knights and even  heavily armored mounted soldiers to  ensure players are engaging these mobs  instead of just circumventing them the  game offers replenishments to your hp  and fp flask if you manage to emerge  victorious it's not always worth the  risk but i often found that i was  inclined to engage mobs knowing that i  had a good chance of regaining recovery  items over the course of the fight this  approach seems to create a good risk  versus reward consideration while also  delivering some of the edge of the seat  combat scenarios we know and love if  single enemies and mobs represent  challenge at the low and mid tier the  more classically souls level of  challenge namely hard was found in the  boss enemies i encountered these  presented the classic loop of being  mercilessly ravaged while trying to  defend myself find the right moment to  strike chip away at a boss's health and  have the good sense not to overextend  lest thou be crushed in an instant  put these foolish animations to rest  what stuck out to me most about these  fights was that from software clearly  has identified that after four souls  games their bosses often fit into  archetypes and as a result there are  effective strategies that can be relied  upon ask any soul's warm player about  how to deal with big lumbering enemies  with swords and there's a good chance  they'll say get up in their booty and  start stabbing for night like opponents  circle strafing and rolling between  their sword swings is generally a decent  starting point however i found that  these strategies were not nearly as  reliable in the fights i had in elden  ring bloodhound dary will for example  was a boss i discovered out in the wild  a strange glyph in a circular arena  transported me to what felt like a  parallel instance of that location and i  was face to face with an incredibly  aggressive enemy despite clearly being  some sort of twisted oversized human  bloodhound dairy wheel moved like a  rabid dog leaping around the arena it  was incredibly aggressive giving me very  little opportunity to create space and  when i did manage to it would swiftly  close the gap and apply pressure i  barely had time to even slip from my hp  flask so using my lightning ability was  a death sentence although the guard  counter allowed me to do some damage the  erratic nature of its attacks meant we  often traded blows and it was doing way  more damage than i could take to rely on  it rolling through attacks and landing  quick strikes was clearly the way to go  but instead i opted to stand my ground  and learn the parry timing it took a few  attempts but eventually i was able to  identify its tails and respond with a  swing of my shield to knock it back and  repulse dealing huge damage with each  onslaught of its attacks the stakes grew  higher as did the pressure but when i  finally landed that final hit the sense  of relation i felt was  well classically from software all these  years later that gameplay loop is as  intoxicating as the first time i  experienced it the other major fight of  the beta i had was against the guardian  of stormville castle who somehow was  even more aggressive despite being huge  margaret moved incredibly fast jumping  at me and swinging his massive sword in  a sweeping motion making it really  difficult to maneuver around him he was  also able to summon ethereal golden  blades that he could throw from a  distance or use as a secondary attack up  close for this fight i had to employ  spirit summons these are executed using  special items and at the cost of fp they  are also only possible in specific  locations where the rebirth monuments  are nearby i called upon a pack of  wolves that while not particularly hardy  when they met margaret's blade gave me  just enough breathing room to get a few  attacks in unchallenged and also back  away from danger to recover health again  this was a situation where my years of  playing soulsborne games created an  expectation of how this fight would go  and how i could approach it but from  software subverted that market was a  satisfying challenge and one of many i  expect  the last major piece of the soulsborne  puzzle death and rebirth has also  undergone some changes for eldon ring in  from software's dark souls games the  penalty for dying was harsh you'd  respawn at a bonfire with all your  currency lost often a considerable  distance away from where you died and be  encouraged to make the same perilous  journey all over again in order to  recover that lost currency however in an  open world this approach could become  incredibly frustrating as players have a  greater freedom of approach  to address this from software has  introduced stakes of marika which are  effectively checkpoints near a major  encounter or area of interest in the  open world if you happen upon an  encampment or a random dungeon chances  are one of the stakes of america are  located nearby there's an on-screen  indicator to confirm this upon death you  are given the choice of either reviving  at the last sight of grace you visited  or the stakes of marika in the area this  option will undoubtedly make the game  less punishing as it won't feel like you  need to go through an uphill struggle  just to get to the starting line again  and it also feels good to be able to  retry a challenge almost immediately  eldon ring is from software's first real  attempt at an open world action game and  based on what i've played so far it  feels like a smart elaboration of the  games they make best it isn't poised to  be a fundamental reinvention of its  gameplay template but it's not one that  i think is going to feel overly familiar  by pushing back the boundaries they've  created a wealth of new opportunities to  explore and the chance to  re-contextualize some older ones the  soul of from software's games the  challenging gameplay specifically is  very much present in eldon ring but from  software looks like it's creating a  stronger body around that core with this  new game there are things that i've not  stopped thinking about since playing  questions that remain unanswered the  beach i accidentally found myself on  where strange silver glyphs were being  stamped into the ground the decrepit  church that i discovered which had a  toppled over statue of a dragon with the  flame still burning in its mouth these  were just a couple of mysteries i  encountered in a very small slice of the  lands between and i had very little  context for how they fit into the  narrative underpinning the game needless  to say i cannot wait to see how it all  comes together and as a fan of from  software's previous games i hope it  lives up to my hopes few things are as  exciting to me as the idea of an open  world souls game  i offer you an accord  [Music]  you

